 "result_text": "I changed `get_adapted_type` in `src/sqlacodegen/generators.py` so MySQL `DOUBLE(11,2)` should no longer come out as `Double(11, True)`. I didn't reproduce the MySQL case against a real database, and I added no test for it. The existing suite passes (94 tests).\n\nThe cause was an exception in that function that lets SQLAlchemy's generic `Float`/`Double` stand in for a dialect type even when the two compile to different SQL. It exists for PostgreSQL, but nothing limited it to that dialect. MySQL's `DOUBLE(11,2)` was adapted to `Double`, whose first two arguments are `precision` and `asdecimal`. The scale of 2 was lost, and the call rendered as `Double(11, True)`.\n\nThe exception no longer applies when the original column type has a scale. In that case the adaptation is rejected, so the dialect-specific type, and its `scale`, is kept as it was.\n\nI haven't checked what the MySQL case now renders as. I expect `DOUBLE(11, 2)` with `precision` and `scale` preserved.",
